<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.8"/>
    <code code="51848-0" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation note"/>
    <title>Assessment</title>
    <text><list><item>Nasal blockage, thick discolored nasal drainage, pressure over the left cheek and forehead, and a reduced sense of smell for about five months, with pressure pain in the left cheek on descent when flying. Anterior rhinoscopy shows swollen, red inferior turbinates without visible polyps or pus; SNOT-22 52. Suspected chronic rhinosinusitis; nasal endoscopy and otolaryngology consultation booked: active, confirmed [S]</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.10" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="18776-5" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Plan of care note"/>
    <title>Plan of Treatment</title>
    <text><list><item>First-line medical therapy: high-volume saline irrigation twice daily and fluticasone nasal spray two sprays in each nostril daily, with technique taught in clinic; topical decongestant sprays stopped because rebound blockage follows use beyond a few days. Guideline: Clinical practice guideline (update): adult sinusitis (2015); International consensus statement on allergy and rhinology: rhinosinusitis 2021 (2021) [S]</item><item>Otolaryngology consultation with nasal endoscopy to confirm the diagnosis with objective evidence and to look for nasal polyps, and advice on further treatment, imaging, and surgery. Guideline: Clinical practice guideline (update): adult sinusitis (2015); International consensus statement on allergy and rhinology: rhinosinusitis 2021 (2021) [S]</item><item>Check for conditions that change management (asthma, cystic fibrosis, immune deficiency, ciliary dyskinesia, aspirin sensitivity, a dental source): history, blood eosinophils and total IgE; no imaging and no antifungal treatment at this stage. Guideline: Clinical practice guideline (update): adult sinusitis (2015); International consensus statement on allergy and rhinology: rhinosinusitis 2021 (2021) [S]</item><item>Fitness to fly: no flying while blocked or in pain, as 14 CFR 61.53 requires; a 48-hour ground trial before flying on any new medicine; only medicines the FAA accepts for pilots (nasal steroid sprays, saline, non-sedating antihistamines, pseudoephedrine); the diphenhydramine night tablet stopped, with at least 60 hours before flying after any dose; and practice of ear and sinus clearing on descent. Guideline: Guide for Aviation Medical Examiners: Over-the-Counter (OTC) Medications Reference Guide, What OTC Medications Can I Take and Still Be Safe to Fly? (2024); Guide for Aviation Medical Examiners: Allergy - Antihistamine and Immunotherapy Medication (2026) [S]</item></list></text>

  </section>
